1 OCCU Sýslumaður á Klofa í Landi.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Torfi Jónsson í Klofa, also known as Torfi ríki ('the powerful/rich') or Torfi stóri ('the great') (c. 1460–1504) was an Icelandic sýslumaður and chieftain of the fifteenth century, a significant figure who had tou
2 CONC gh dealings with Stefán Jónsson, Bishop of Skálholt, and had Lénharð fógeti killed in 1502. Torfi's life is reported in a number of folktales as well as contemporary records, and fact in his life is sometimes hard to distinguish f
2 CONC rom legend.
2 CONT
2 CONT Torfi, who lived in Hvammur í Dölum and Hjörsey in Mýrar before he moved to Klofi, also had disputes with the bishop, both because Torfi protected people from prosecution by the bishop and also the bishop did not think he paid all
2 CONC  of his tithes and other taxes to the Church. Torfi seems to have been a major troublemaker, who would stop at nothing. It is, however, said that relations between Torfi and the bishop settled down after Torfi was struck with illn
2 CONC ess at the Alþingi, giving a great cry and becoming so strong that it took eight men to hold him and tied him up. The bishop had the priests calm him with readings and singing until Torfi slept and thereafter was less troublesome.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1502 Torfi had Lénharður fógeti killed. Little is known about Lénharður, a foreign thief based at Arnarbæli. Torfi pursued him with a posse and had him killed at Hraun í Ölfusi. Torfi confessed this to Bishop Stefán but the bis
2 CONC hop did not impose a major penance, considering the killing a public service.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the Alþingi in 1504, Torfi was again being aggressive, wanting to drive the sýslumaður Arnór Finnsson from office, influencing the judgement with a band of armed men. Not long afterwards, Torfi died. There are two accounts of h
2 CONC is death: he is generally said to have died from sickness at Skíðbakki in Landeyjar, or from drinking at Fíflholtsþing. After his death, his widow made peace with Bishop Stefán, handing over three farms and a silver cross for Torf
2 CONC i to be buried at Skálholt, as he had wished.
2 CONT
2 CONT Torfi's wife was Helga Guðnadóttir (d. 1544), daughter of the sýslumaður Guðni Jónsson of Kirkjuból, the brother of Torfi's stepfather Ormur Jónsson, og his wife Þóra, the daughter of Björn Þorleifsson hirðstjóri of Skarð. Helga a
2 CONC nd Torfi had many children, most of whom were still children when their father died. Helga lived as a widow at Klofi until 1525.
2 CONT
2 CONT Appearances in popular culture[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Torfi allegedly gives his name to Torfajökull where, according to legend, he found a hidden valley where took his people in 1493 to escape the plague.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of Torfi's exploits are recounted and utilised in Kristín Helga Gunnarsdóttir's children's novel Ríólítreglan (Reykjavík: Mál og Menning, 2011).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources[edit] 'Þar harpan bannar börnunum að sofa', Sunnudagsblað Tímans, 3. sept. 1967, http://timarit.is/view_page_init.jsp?pageId=3556433. http://www.snerpa.is/net/thjod/torfi.htm
