1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''MAry Fulford Achym Gorges'''
2 CONT '''Birth	1586'''
2 CONT '''Death	1 Aug 1623 (aged 36–37)'''
2 CONT '''London, City of London, Greater London, England'''
2 CONT '''Burial	Burial Details Unknown'''
2 CONT Find a Grave Memorial ID: 240659569
2 CONT lineage at https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Fulford-465
2 CONT Family Members
2 CONT '''Parents'''
2 CONT Sir Thomas Fulford
2 CONT 1553–1610
2 CONT
2 CONT Ursula Bamfield Fulford
2 CONT unknown–1639
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Spouses'''
2 CONT Thomas Achym
2 CONT 1570–1618 (m. 1608)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Ferdinando Gorges
2 CONT 1568–1647 (m. 1621)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Siblings'''
2 CONT Bridget Fulford Champernowne
2 CONT 1578 – unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel Francis Fulford
2 CONT 1583–1664
2 CONT From: https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/240659569/mary_achym_gorges
2 CONT _____________
2 CONT *'''Sir Ferdinando Gorges (1565–1647), the "Father of English Colonization in North America",[1] was an early English colonial entrepreneur and founder of the Province of Maine in 1622, although Gorges himself never set foot in th
2 CONC e New World.
2 CONT *'''Sir Ferdinando Gorges''' was born in July 1565 in Clerkenwell, Middlesex, England, the son of Edward Gorges, Esquire and Lady Cicely Lygon. He was born only a few weeks before his father's death. He is named for his mother's b
2 CONC rother, Ferdinando Lygon. Very little documentation exists regarding his early life and education. He was brought up at Nailsea Court at Kenn near Wraxall.[2] He is descended from a cadet branch of the Russells of Kingston Russell
2 CONC , Dorset, which had changed its name to the matronymic Gorges, which family had died out in the male line on the death of Ralph de Gorges of Knighton, Isle of Wight, 2nd Baron Gorges, in 1331.
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *'''Ferdinando Gorges's''' son was Robert Gorges, Governor-General of New England from 1623–1624. But Robert Gorges was seen with some suspicion by American colonists, who were skeptical of Gorges' almost feudal idea of governance
2 CONC  and settlement, and ultimately Gorges returned to England. In the 1630s Gorges attempted to revive the moribund claims of the Plymouth Company. In concert with colonists banished from the Massachusetts Bay Colony, he formally que
2 CONC stioned the issuance of its royal charter in 1632, and forwarded complaints and charges made by the disaffected colonists to the Privy Council of Charles I. His efforts were ultimately unsuccessful.[15]
2 CONT *'''Sir Ferdinando Gorges''' died on May 24, 1647[16] in his home in Long Ashton (then known as Ashton-Phillips) and is buried in the All Saint's Churchyard, Long Ashton, Somerset, England. He is buried in the Smyth crypt without 
2 CONC markings due the circumstances of the time.[17] His eldest son, John, inherited his Province of Maine, of which Robert, his younger son, had been for such a short time Governor. In May 1677 his grandson, another Ferdinando, finall
2 CONC y sold to Massachusetts all rights to Maine for £1,250.
2 CONT *The epilogue to '''Sir Ferdinando Gorges' story is very brief'''. Although his grandson eventually accepted a paltry sum after many years of trying to secure the good name of his grandfather, he proceeded to acquire some validity
2 CONC  of his grandfather's claims by the Puritans. This sale finally extinguished the interests of the Gorges family in those American lands which Sir Ferdinando had labored to develop as a proprietary province owing to a close relatio
2 CONC nship to the English Crown. New England was left to follow a very different destiny from that which Sir Ferdinando had devoted so much of his life.[18] It wasn't until 1820 that Maine achieved separate statehood.[19]
2 CONT *'''He married four times'''. His first wife was Ann, daughter of Edward Bell of Writtle, Essex, whom he married in 1589 at St. Margaret's, Westminster, and who died in 1620: they had two sons, John and Robert, and two daughters, 
2 CONC Ellen and Honoria, the last of whom died young. '''Secondly, in 1621, Mary, daughter''' of Thomas Fulford of Devon, '''the widow of Thomas Achims of Pelint, Cornwall.''' Thirdly, in 1627, at Ladock, Cornwall, to Elizabeth, daughte
2 CONC r of Tristam Gorges of St. Budeaux, and widow of Edward Courteney and of William Bligh (she died within a few weeks of the marriage). Fourthly, at Wraxall in 1629, to Elizabeth, Lady Smyth, daughter of Sir Thomas Gorges and Helena
2 CONC , Marchioness of Northampton, and widow of Sir Hugh Smyth of Ashton Court.[3]
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinando_Gorges 
2 CONT ______________________
2 CONT *'''GORGES, Sir Ferdinando''' (c.1568-1647), of Plymouth, Devon; later of Ashton Phillips, Som.
2 CONT *b. c.1568, 2nd s. of Edward Gorges of Wraxall, Som. by Cecily, da. of William Lygon of Worcs. m. (1) 1589, Anne, da. of Edward Ball of Writtle, Essex; '''(2) Mary, da.''' of Thomas Fulford, '''wid. of Thomas Achim of Hall, Cornw.
2 CONC '''; (3) 1637, Elizabeth, da. of Tristram Gorges of St. Budeaux, Devon; (4) Elizabeth, da. of Hugh Smith, 2s. 2da. Kntd. 1591.
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1558-1603/member/gorges-sir-ferdinando-1568-1647 
2 CONT _________________
2 CONT *'''Sir Ferdinando Gorges and his province of Maine. Including the Brief relations, the Brief narration, his defence, the charter granted to him, his will, and his letters (1890) Vol. 2
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/sirferdinandogo02baxt
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/sirferdinandogo02baxt#page/165/mode/1up
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *   18. EDWARD GORGES.  He was born about 1537, and married Cicely,450 daughter of William Lygon, of Madres-
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/sirferdinandogo02baxt#page/166/mode/1up
2 CONT *field, Worcefterfhire. .... he died at the age of thirty-one years. His death took place at Clerkenwell, in the fuburbs of London, Auguft 29th, 1568, and he was buried there in the parifh church of St. James. He left two young ch
2 CONC ildren, Edward 451 and '''Ferdinando Gorges (19)''', the latter of whom has attained celebrity largely on account of his efforts in eftablifhing colonies in New England; indeed, he has been termed the "Father of American Colonizat
2 CONC ion."
2 CONT *'''   19. Sir Ferdinando Gorges''' was the nineteenth in defcent from Ranolph the Norman, and, as we have feen, defcended from a long line of notable anceftors,  .... 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/sirferdinandogo02baxt#page/167/mode/1up
2 CONT * ....   '''He was married, as has been before ftated, four times'''; firft on February 24th, 1589, at St. Margaret's, Weftminfter, to Ann, daughter of Edward Bell, Efq., of Writtle, Effex, and by her had four children ; namely, J
2 CONC ohn (20), Robert, Ellen, and Honoria. The two daughters died young. The Lady Ann died Auguft 26th, 1620, and was buried at St. Sepulchre's, London. '''His fecond marriage was to Mary, fifter''' of Sir Francis Fulford, knight, and 
2 CONC '''relict of Thomas Achim, Efq., of Hall, Cornwall, who died in 1619. Lady Mary Gorges died in 1622.''' Sir Ferdinando married thirdly, ....
2 CONT ___________________
